---

<IndividualPageLayout
    title={seoTitle}
    description={seoDescription}
    modifiedDate={dates.modified}
    publishedDate = {dates.published}
    pageTitle={pageTitle}
    heroImage="/media/industry-aerospace-turbine-engine-v5.webp"
    heroImageAlt="industry-aerospace-turbine-engine"
    faqSectionTitle="FAQ: Sourcing Inconel 625 casting"
    faqItems={faqs}
    ctaTitle="Request an Inconel 625 Technical Quote"
    ctaDescription="Our Delaware-based engineering team serves as your direct link to our co-owned global manufacturing network. Partner with an industry leader for your high-performance alloy needs."
    sourcesTitle="Sources"
    sourcesList={sources}
    resourcesTitle="Our Internal Resources for Die Casting, Investment casting, Forging and Sand Casting"
    resourcesList={internalResources}
>
    <div class="individual-content ">
        <p>
            <strong>Inconel 625 Casting Key Facts:</strong> Inconel 625 (UNS N06625) is a nickel-chromium-molybdenum-niobium alloy valued for resistance to pitting, crevice corrosion, and many chloride-containing environments. Vacuum investment castings may be produced to applicable specifications such as AMS 5401. ForceBeyond supports VIM for applicable casting routes, HIP where required, heat treatment, NDT, and precision machining. Published property data extend across cryogenic and elevated-temperature conditions, while actual allowable service conditions depend on material condition, geometry, loading, environment, code requirements, and the governing specification.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div slot="full-width" class="individual-content">

        <section id="overview">
            <div class="content-container">
                
                <h2>Inconel 625 Casting: Corrosion-Resistant Solutions for Nuclear & Marine</h2>

                <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-center mt-6">
                    <div>
                        <p>
                           ForceBeyond is a premier <strong>Inconel 625 casting supplier</strong> with co-owned foundries in the USA, South Korea, 
                           and Taiwan. We support <strong>Vacuum Induction Melting (VIM)</strong> for applicable casting routes and <strong>Hot Isostatic Pressing (HIP)</strong> where required to control internal porosity. Our manufacturing model coordinates engineering and quality oversight across the network for marine, subsea oil and gas, chemical-processing, and selected <strong>Nuclear / SMR</strong> programs where the alloy and casting route are permitted by the applicable code, specification, qualification plan, and customer requirements. Learn more about our advanced 
                           VIM and quality control processes on our <a href="/high-performance-superalloy-casting/" class="highlight-link">
                                Superalloy Casting Services
                            </a> hub.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="relative">
                        <div class="overflow-hidden">
                            <img
                                src="/media/Inconel-625-gas-turbine-disc-via-VIM-at-ForceBeyond-co-owned-foundry-for-aerospace-power-generation.webp"
                                alt="Inconel-625-gas-turbine-disc-via-VIM-at-ForceBeyond-co-owned-foundry-for-aerospace-power-generation"
                                class="w-full h-auto object-cover hover:scale-105 transition-transform duration-500"
                            />
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="properties-comparison">
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Inconel 625 Casting Properties vs. Inconel 718</h2>
                
                <p>
                    Selecting the correct superalloy is critical for component longevity. 
                    While <a href="/inconel-718-casting" class="highlight-link">Inconel 718 Casting</a> is the preferred choice for high-temperature mechanical strength and creep resistance, 
                    <strong>Inconel 625</strong> is selected for its superior resistance to pitting, crevice corrosion, 
                    and chloride-ion stress-corrosion cracking.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Corrosion Resistance:</strong> Strong resistance to seawater, chloride-containing environments, and many chemical-processing media, subject to concentration, temperature, material condition, and service conditions.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Broad Temperature Capability:</strong> Published property data cover cryogenic and elevated-temperature conditions. Allowable service temperature and fatigue performance depend on material condition, loading, environment, geometry, and governing specification.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Fabricability:</strong> Alloy 625 is widely used in welded and fabricated corrosion-resistant systems; welding procedure, heat treatment, qualification, and post-weld requirements remain application- and specification-dependent.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="properties-table" >
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Technical Specifications: Precision Inconel 625 Castings</h2>
                    <p>
                        Inconel 625 vacuum investment castings may be produced to applicable
                        casting specifications such as <strong>AMS 5401</strong>, subject to the
                        required alloy condition, drawing, customer specification, heat treatment,
                        inspection requirements, and specified revision.
                    </p>
                <div class="content-table-wrapper">
                    <table>

                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col">Property</th>
                                <th scope="col">Typical Value (Cast & Annealed)</th>

                            </tr>
                        </thead>

                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Tensile strength</th>
                                <td>120,000 – 150,000 psi (827 – 1034 MPa)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Yield strength(0.2% offset)</th>
                                <td>60,000 – 95,000 psi (414 – 655 MPa)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Density</th>
                                <td>0.305 lb/in³ (8.44 g/cm³)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Melting Range</th>
                                <td>2350 – 2460°F (1290 – 1350°C)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="Tier-1.5-Integration" >
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Tier-1.5 Integration: From Complex Casting to Final Print</h2>
                
                <p>
                    Because Inconel 625 is highly ductile and “gummy,” it presents unique challenges during secondary machining. 
                    ForceBeyond’s Tier-1.5 model integrates our vacuum foundries with our specialized <a href="/inconel-625-machining" class="highlight-link">Inconel 625 Machining</a> 
                    centers in the <strong>USA</strong>, <strong>South Korea</strong>, and <strong>Taiwan</strong>.
                </p>
                
                <p>
                    By coordinating the component from the initial pour through 5-axis CNC finishing, ForceBeyond can reduce avoidable hand-off risk and control critical features through one manufacturing plan. Sealing surfaces and datum features are finish-machined to drawing-defined tolerances subject to geometry, size, feature accessibility, inspection method, and process-capability review.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For high-pressure components requiring optimized grain flow and superior 
                    fatigue strength, explore our integrated <a href="/inconel-625-forging" class="highlight-link">Inconel 625 Forging</a> capabilities.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="mission-critical-applications" >
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Mission-Critical Applications for Cast Inconel 625</h2>
                
                <p>
                   Our co-owned foundry network supports global infrastructure with certified Inconel 625 hardware:
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Nuclear & SMR:</strong> Selected corrosion-resistant components where Inconel 625 casting is permitted by the applicable code, material specification, qualification plan, service conditions, and customer requirements.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Marine & Subsea:</strong> Seawater piping systems, propeller shafts, and subsea cable connectors.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Chemical Processing:</strong> Reaction vessels, heat exchanger tubing, and valves for acidic environments.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Aerospace:</strong> Jet engine exhaust systems, fuel manifolds, and bellows.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="advanced-post-processing" >
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Advanced Post-Processing: HIP for Internal Porosity Control</h2>
                <p>
                    <strong>Hot Isostatic Pressing (HIP)</strong> may be applied to selected Inconel 625 castings to reduce internal microporosity and increase density. HIP is used when required by the drawing, material specification, qualification plan, customer requirements, or service conditions; it does not provide a universal 100% density or zero-defect guarantee.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Porosity Reduction:</strong> HIP can close or reduce certain internal voids when the defect morphology and alloy condition are suitable.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fatigue Performance:</strong> Final fatigue life depends on material condition, defects, heat treatment, geometry, surface condition, residual stress, inspection acceptance criteria, and service environment.</li>
                    <li><strong>VIM Process Control:</strong> VIM helps limit oxidation, gas pickup, and contamination during melting of nickel-based superalloys.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>


        <section id="why-choose-forcebeyond" >
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Why Choose ForceBeyond as Your Inconel 625 Supplier?</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>VIM (Vacuum Induction Melting):</strong> For applicable casting routes, VIM helps limit oxidation, gas pickup, and contamination during melting, supporting alloy cleanliness and consistency.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Co-Owned Supply Chain Redundancy:</strong> We operate our own facilities in the <strong>USA, South Korea, and Taiwan</strong>. This allows us to mitigate geopolitical risks and provide prioritized lead times for mission-critical projects.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Advanced NDT & HIP:</strong> Inspection and HIP requirements are established from the drawing, material specification, qualification plan, customer requirements, and service conditions. HIP may be used to reduce internal microporosity; final acceptance remains based on the applicable inspection criteria.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="quality-compliance" >
            <div class="content-container">
                <h2>Quality Compliance & Corrosion Verification</h2>
                <p>
                    Inspection requirements are established from the applicable drawing, material specification, qualification plan, corrosion requirements, and customer acceptance criteria. NDT and material verification are used to detect relevant discontinuities and confirm compliance; they do not constitute a universal zero-defect guarantee.
                </p>
                <div class="content-table-wrapper">
                    <table>

                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col">Inspection Method</th>
                                <th scope="col">Purpose for Inconel 625 Casting</th>
                                <th scope="col">Specification Compliance</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>

                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">X-Ray (Radiography)</th>
                                <td>Detecting internal shrinkage in complex valve bodies.</td>
                                <td>ASTM E192 / ASTM E1030</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Corrosion Testing</th>
                                <td>Verifying resistance to intergranular corrosion.</td>
                                <td>ASTM G28 Methods A & B</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Positive Material ID</th>
                                <td>Ensuring precise Ni-Cr-Mo metallurgical balance.</td>
                                <td>Spectrographic Analysis</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">Fluorescent Penetrant</th>
                                <td>Surface-breaking crack detection post-machining.</td>
                                <td>ASTM E1417 or applicable customer requirement</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</IndividualPageLayout>